{
  "headline": "Oil pipeline damaged in sabotage attack in Syria’s Hasakah Governorate",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "An oil pipeline near Tel Hamis village in northeastern Syria’s Hasakah Governorate was damaged in a sabotage attack on Wednesday, according to the Syrian Petroleum Company (SPC). The SPC stated that its maintenance and emergency teams traveled to the site, secured the area, and isolated the damaged section before beginning repairs. This incident follows other recent attacks on Syria’s energy infrastructure, marking the third such occurrence in recent weeks."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Parti Québécois wins election in Quebec, Canada, but falls short of majority",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Parti Québécois (PQ) won the Quebec election on October 5, 2026, gaining its first power in 14 years by adding 56 seats to its roster and more than doubling its vote share from the 2022 election. Despite the victory, the party fell five seats short of a majority, which complicates its promise to hold a referendum on separating the French-speaking province from Canada after Donald Trump leaves office in 2028. According to polling analyst Philippe Fournier, the PQ won with approximately 28% of the popular vote, the lowest percentage for any government in Canadian electoral history. The Guardian World reports that the vast majority of Quebec voters chose parties that do not seek a referendum, reflecting a 30-year aversion to such debates, particularly following trade tensions and tariffs from the Trump administration. The lack of a majority also impacts other PQ initiatives, such as plans to abolish the monarchy, apply strict language laws to provincial colleges, and reduce immigration levels, a move that has drawn opposition from the business community and farmers."
    },
    {
      "text": "PQ leader Paul St-Pierre Plamondon addressed the crowd in English during his victory speech, pledging to be a “good-faith collaborator” with the rest of Canada. On October 6, 2026, he announced he would prevent the lieutenant governor, representing the British crown, from swearing in his new government. The political landscape remains influenced by Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney, who remains a popular figure in the province due to his resistance to Trump. The election results suggest that while the PQ has returned to power, the path toward provincial separation remains uncertain."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Study finds land use changes after colonisation created a wildfire loop in south-east Australia",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Researchers have identified a “catastrophic wildfire loop” in south-east Australia, where land use changes following colonisation have increased susceptibility to climate-fuelled bushfires. According to a study published in Nature, the shift from open woodland to dense forest after 1788 led to more intense fires, a change consistent with the removal of the Gunaikurnai people from the land and the subsequent loss of cultural burning practices. Michael-Shawn Fletcher, a professor at the University of Melbourne and the study’s lead author, stated that land use changes occurred more than a century before climate changes, allowing shrubs to increase and fires to become hotter. These intense fires then trigger eucalypt recruitment, which in turn drives further fire. The research was conducted by a team working with the Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation, an organisation representing traditional owners. The team reconstructed 1,000 years of vegetation and fire history at Purghagoolah, a lake in Croajingolong National Park in Gippsland, Victoria, by analysing pollen and charcoal particles in a metre-long sediment core. Fletcher noted that prior to colonisation, the area was a “cared for and managed landscape.” Cultural burning involves First Nations people using low-temperature fire to maintain landscapes through a “right time, right place” approach, which differs from government hazard reduction practices. While these practices are still used in parts of Australia as of October 7, 2026, Fletcher suggested the research provides scientific evidence for integrating Indigenous knowledge into modern fire management. Adam Morton, a fire scientist at the University of Tasmania who was not an author of the paper, described the research as significant to the understanding of landscape change resulting from the loss of Indigenous fire management."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Study finds scientists with high faith in technology less likely to take climate action",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A study from the London School of Economics, published in Environmental Research Letters, found that scientists who believe technology will largely solve the problems caused by the climate crisis are less likely to engage in civic action or lifestyle changes. The research surveyed more than 9,000 scientists across 115 countries regarding their views on “techno-optimism.” Of those surveyed, 27% agreed that technology will largely solve climate change problems, while 44% disagreed and 29% neither agreed nor disagreed. The report found that techno-optimism was most prevalent among scientists working in applied and natural sciences and those with right-leaning political views. Lead author Fabian Dablander stated that the belief in technological solutions can lead to an over-reliance on unproven solutions at the expense of necessary socioeconomic transformations, noting that technological solutions are often politically attractive because they do not require changes to how people live and consume."
    },
    {
      "text": "According to the London School of Economics report, “techno-optimist” scientists are 23% less likely to sign petitions or participate in protests than peers who doubt technology is the answer. These scientists also reported engaging in less advocacy and fewer civic actions. Additionally, the study found that techno-optimists are 18% less likely to make high-impact lifestyle changes, such as reducing flights, cutting car usage, or adopting a mostly vegan or vegetarian diet. Dablander noted that while technology cannot substitute for broader societal changes and large-scale shifts in consumption, there is a tendency at the individual level for greater faith in technological solutions to go hand in hand with less engagement in climate action."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Former Tottenham Hotspur defender Ramon Vega intends to run for FIFA president",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ramon Vega, a 55-year-old former Switzerland centre-back who played for Tottenham Hotspur, Celtic, and Watford, has announced his intention to stand as a candidate in next year's FIFA presidential election. Vega is the first person to confirm a challenge to current president Gianni Infantino. In a statement, Vega said he is standing to reunite the global football family, deliver reform, modernize FIFA, and rebuild trust in the game. He added that his life has prepared him to serve the sport, which he described as belonging to players, coaches, supporters, and communities rather than administrators. Since retiring from football in 2004, Vega has worked in finance. He previously considered running for the position in 2015 following the suspension of former president Sepp Blatter, and expressed intent to stand against Infantino four years later, though he did not secure the backing of five of FIFA's 211 member federations required to be included in the ballot. To qualify, candidates must be proposed by a member association, have played an active role in the game for two of the past five years, and pass an eligibility check by a review committee."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Annual job creation in Bangladesh lags behind labor market entry, World Bank report finds",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Between 2016 and 2024, approximately 2 million people entered the labor market in Bangladesh each year, while only about 900,000 new jobs were created annually, according to a special chapter in the World Bank's 2026 Bangladesh Development update report. The report, titled “What Business Environment is Needed to Boost Employment,” notes that seven out of every 10 new jobs were in agriculture, while employment in the industrial sector decreased. For women, the report states that while 4.2 million new jobs were created in agriculture, industrial jobs decreased by 1.1 million and service sector jobs decreased by 800,000. Consequently, female labor force participation dropped from 42.8% in 2022 to 38.4% in 2024."
    },
    {
      "text": "The World Bank assessment describes a “productivity paradox” in Bangladesh, noting that while the economy grew at an average annual rate of 6% from fiscal year 2015-16 to 2024-25, progress in productivity, investment, and new business creation at the firm level has not kept pace. The report indicates that the top 10% of firms by productivity control 75% of total sales in the formal sector but account for only 15% of employment. Other businesses face obstacles including complex government procedures, lack of financing, unfair competition, and power shortages. Senior managers spend an average of 13% of their time dealing with government regulations, and 71% of firms experience power outages, which costs them approximately 9% of their annual sales. The report draws on data from the World Bank Enterprise Survey 2022, Business Ready 2025, Business of the State 2019, Bangladesh Country Private Sector Diagnostic 2025, and UNCTAD investment data."
    }
  ]
}
